  "summary": "Palo Alto City Council discussed Flock Safety's nationwide lookup feature that allowed out-of-state searches of the city's ALPR data without permission, violating California's SB 34 and city policy. Council members expressed distrust of Flock and its CEO, with the mayor seeking a financial penalty, while police defended the cameras' crime-solving value. The council has tasked a police auditor to investigate; the contract runs through December 2029.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Flock allowed nationwide searches of Palo Alto's camera data without the city's knowledge or permission, Chief James Reifschneider revealed in an April 21 statement.",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "I'm just not sure about being able to trust this company going forward",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "Why does my trip to the supermarket earn me a spot in a government database controlled by a company that so recklessly disregards state law and indeed your own robust policies?",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "Still, council is having a police auditor look into Flock. The city's contract with Flock ends in December 2029.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    }
  ],
